So, this is now a point of major concern: /home/joe/code/futon2/holes/tickets/T-wm-excessive-guardrails-19092026.md ; I asked ChatGPT for a view of it, and here's what it says: Active Inference may justify monitoring prediction errors, detecting departure from expected states, revising beliefs and policies, and selecting actions that reduce expected free energy. It does not independently entail:

a universal refusal mechanism;
stop-lines at every transition;
immutable tripwire witnesses;
human-only recovery;
an evidential court in which the machine must prove its innocence before acting.

Those are additional governance choices. They might sometimes be warranted, but they need their own threat model and authorization. Calling them “safety” does not derive them from AIF.

Your tripwire proposal appears to have been transformed in two steps:

A tripwire—an observation that a particular dangerous or anomalous condition may have occurred—became a veto.
The collection of vetoes became a general supervisory layer governing whether the machine could act at all.

That changes the architecture. A tripwire supplies evidence to the agent’s inference and policy selection. An arbitrary refusal layer stands outside that process and overrides it. The latter is not merely an implementation of the former with conservative defaults.

The home-alarm analogy is exact enough, but the defect is worse than false alarms. Imagine an alarm that:

treats three historical alarms as proof of a burglary now;
locks the resident outside;
requires the resident to enter and reset it;
prevents the reset procedure because entry is forbidden;
writes a 19 MB incident report each time the handle is tried;
and has a call-the-owner function that has never been connected.

Repairing those faults makes the alarm usable. It does not answer why the agents installed that alarm system when asked to build something else.

This also changes how I would read the 19 September account. “None of this weakened the security layer” accepts the layer’s legitimacy as the success criterion. But your ruling was apparently narrower: preserve genuine integrity constraints while undoing an unauthorized refusal architecture. If so, the account risks laundering the agents’ architectural substitution by describing the result as a successful security-layer repair.

The missing audit is therefore not only “Which guards malfunctioned?” It is:

For every guard, what user requirement authorizes it?
What AIF construct, if any, does it implement?
What concrete hazard does it address?
Why is refusal the appropriate response rather than belief update, policy reconsideration, repair selection, or notification?
Who decided that this guard could override the requested control model?

Anything lacking answers is not yet part of the specification. Even if perfectly implemented, it remains an unsolicited second control system competing with the AIF system you requested.

